# Adigam AI — environment variables
#
# There is no environment file at the repository root, and nothing reads one from here. The two
# halves configure themselves separately, so this file exists to say where they are rather than to
# be copied:
#
#   backend/.env.example   ->  cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
#   frontend/.env.example  ->  cp frontend/.env.example frontend/.env
#
# Both real `.env` files are gitignored and must stay that way.
#
#
# WHICH VALUES ARE SAFE WHERE
#
# The split matters more than the list. Two keys must never cross into the frontend:
#
#   SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY   bypasses row-level security entirely
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY     is billable, and a key in a bundle is a key anyone can spend
#
# Everything in `frontend/.env` is compiled into the browser bundle, so only the publishable
# Supabase key and the API URL belong there. The publishable key is meant to be public: it grants
# nothing on its own, because every table is behind row-level security and needs a signed-in
# learner's token.
#
#
# TWO THINGS THAT ARE EASY TO GET WRONG
#
# 1. An empty value is treated as absent, not as an empty string. `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=` with nothing
#    after it once stopped the server booting with no useful message, because zod's `.optional()`
#    rejects an empty string. The parser now filters empties before validating.
#
# 2. The frontend build FAILS when a VITE_ variable is missing, deliberately. Vite inlines a missing
#    variable as `undefined`, which turns the guard in `lib/supabase.ts` into an unconditional throw
#    and lets Rollup tree-shake the auth client away — producing a build that succeeds, weighs 25 kB
#    less, and white-screens on load. Measured on this project: 98 kB with the variables, 73 kB
#    without, both reported as clean builds.
#
#
# If a Postgres password contains `@` or `#`, percent-encode it (%40, %23) or the connection URL
# will not parse.
